      "text": "To find the real value of \\( v \\) such that \\(\\frac{-21-\\sqrt{301}}{10}\\) is a root of the quadratic equation \\( 5x^2 + 21x + v \\), we can use the fact that if \\(\\alpha\\) is a root of the quadratic equation \\( ax^2 + bx + c \\), then it satisfies the equation \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\). Here, \\(\\alpha = \\frac{-21-\\sqrt{301}}{10}\\), so it must satisfy:\n\n\\[ 5x^2 + 21x + v = 0. \\]\n\nFirst, substitute \\( x = \\frac{-21-\\sqrt{301}}{10} \\) into the quadratic equation:\n\n\\[ 5 \\left( \\frac{-21-\\sqrt{301}}{10} \\right)^2 + 21 \\left( \\frac{-21-\\sqrt{301}}{10} \\right) + v = 0. \\]\n\nSimplify the expression inside the equation step by step. First, calculate \\( \\left( \\frac{-21-\\sqrt{301}}{10} \\right)^2 \\):\n\n\\[ \\left( \\frac{-21-\\sqrt{301}}{10} \\right)^2 = \\frac{(-21-\\sqrt{301})^2}{100} = \\frac{441 + 42\\sqrt{301} + 301}{100} = \\frac{742 + 42\\sqrt{301}}{100}. \\]\n\nSo the first term becomes:\n\n\\[ 5 \\left( \\frac{742 + 42\\sqrt{301}}{100} \\right) = \\frac{5(742 + 42\\sqrt{301})}{100} = \\frac{3710 + 210\\sqrt{301}}{100} = \\frac{3710}{100} + \\frac{210\\sqrt{301}}{100} = 37.1 + \\frac{21\\sqrt{301}}{10}. \\]\n\nNext, calculate the second term:\n\n\\[ 21 \\left( \\frac{-21-\\sqrt{301}}{10} \\right) = \\frac{21(-21 - \\sqrt{301})}{10} = \\frac{-441 - 21\\sqrt{301}}{10} = -44.1 - \\frac{21\\sqrt{301}}{10}. \\]\n\nPutting it all together, we get:\n\n\\[ 37.1 + \\frac{21\\sqrt{301}}{10} - 44.1 - \\frac{21\\sqrt{301}}{10} + v = 0. \\]\n\nThe terms involving \\(\\sqrt{301}\\) cancel each other out, leaving us with:\n\n\\[ 37.1 - 44.1 + v = 0, \\]\n\\[ -7 + v = 0, \\]\n\\[ v = 7. \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( v \\) is \\(\\boxed{7}\\).",
